C++ Single Inheritance – Person and Student

Description

This C++ program demonstrates single inheritance using two classes: "Person" and "Student".

The "Student" class inherits properties and functions from the "Person" class.

Classes Used

1. Person Class

The "Person" class contains:

- "name" – Stores the person's name.
- "displayName()" – Displays the person's name.
- A constructor to initialize the name.

The "name" variable is declared as "protected", so it can be accessed by the derived "Student" class.

2. Student Class

The "Student" class inherits from the "Person" class using public inheritance.

It contains:

- "rollNumber" – Stores the student's roll number.
- "displayStudent()" – Displays the student's name and roll number.

The "Student" constructor calls the "Person" constructor to initialize the student's name.

Main Function

In the "main()" function:

- A "Student" object named "student" is created.
- The name ""Amit"" and roll number "101" are passed to the constructor.
- "displayStudent()" is called to display the details.

Output

Name: Amit
Roll Number: 101

OOP Concept

Single Inheritance

Person
   |
   ↓
Student

Here, "Person" is the base class, and "Student" is the derived class.
